Read varchar-cast leaves inside a composite instead of refusing the scan.

Some Postgres types are read as their text form rather than as the structured
value upstream would produce: the builtin geometric types (see
geometric-types-as-varchar.patch) and anything the scanner cannot map at all.
Until now that was only possible for a top-level column or an array element,
because Postgres cannot cast a composite as a whole and the scan bailed out with
"cast to varchar not implemented for composite column".  A table with, say, a
composite holding a point was therefore unreadable, even though pg_lake stores
that field as a string in the Iceberg struct and the pg_lake_replication
change-log decoder happily writes it.

Instead of refusing, rebuild the composite server-side field by field with ROW()
and cast only the leaves that need it, recursing through nested composites.  The
binary record Postgres then sends has one field per struct child, which is what
the scanner's struct reader expects, and it ignores the per-field type OIDs.

A NULL composite has to stay NULL: ROW() over the fields of a NULL composite
would produce a row of NULLs, and a composite whose fields are all NULL is not
itself NULL, so the CASE compares the text form, which is NULL only for a NULL
composite.

An array of composites cannot be expressed this way -- the cast would have to be
applied per element -- and that case now throws.  It used to fall through and
scan the column untouched, which handed the binary form of the leaf to the
varchar reader and returned junk, so an error is what it should have been.

--- a/src/postgres_scanner.cpp
+++ b/src/postgres_scanner.cpp
@@ -236,6 +236,44 @@
 	return false;
 }
 
+// Build a Postgres expression that reads a composite column that has leaves which
+// must be read as text.  Postgres cannot cast a composite as a whole, so the
+// composite is rebuilt field by field with ROW() and only the leaves that need it
+// are cast.  The CASE keeps a NULL composite NULL: ROW() over the fields of a NULL
+// composite yields a row of NULLs, and a composite whose fields are all NULL is not
+// itself NULL, so the text cast is what tells the two apart.
+static string BuildVarcharCastProjection(const string &expr, const LogicalType &type,
+                                         const PostgresType &postgres_type, const string &table_name,
+                                         const string &column_name) {
+	if (postgres_type.info == PostgresTypeAnnotation::CAST_TO_VARCHAR) {
+		return expr + "::VARCHAR";
+	}
+	if (!ContainsCastToVarchar(postgres_type)) {
+		return expr;
+	}
+	if (type.id() != LogicalTypeId::STRUCT) {
+		// An array of composites would need the cast applied per element.
+		throw NotImplementedException("Error reading table \"%s\" - cast to varchar not implemented below column "
+		                              "\"%s\" (type %s)",
+		                              table_name, column_name, type.ToString());
+	}
+	auto &children = StructType::GetChildTypes(type);
+	if (children.size() != postgres_type.children.size()) {
+		throw InternalException("Mismatch in struct child count while building the varchar cast for column \"%s\"",
+		                        column_name);
+	}
+	string row_args;
+	for (idx_t c = 0; c < children.size(); c++) {
+		if (c > 0) {
+			row_args += ", ";
+		}
+		auto child_expr = "(" + expr + ")." + KeywordHelper::WriteQuoted(children[c].first, '"');
+		row_args += BuildVarcharCastProjection(child_expr, children[c].second, postgres_type.children[c], table_name,
+		                                       column_name);
+	}
+	return "CASE WHEN (" + expr + ")::TEXT IS NULL THEN NULL ELSE ROW(" + row_args + ") END";
+}
